The night train hissed through the darkness, its rhythm steady as a heartbeat. Conductor Elias leaned against the brass rail, eyes scanning the dimly lit platform as the last station lights flickered out. When the whistle blew, he turned to check the final car, and there—nestled between the sleeper and the dining car—sat a carriage he had never seen. Its paint was a deep, midnight blue, the windows darkened, and a faint, warm glow leaked from the seams. No nameplate, no number, just an inexplicable weight of silence that seemed to pulse with the train’s own breath. He stepped closer, feeling the cool metal under his palm, and wondered why the engine had not announced it. The rails stretched ahead, endless, as the train rolled onward into the night.

